1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,712
A developer wants a documentation change
merged today, and the docs team is slow.

2
00:00:09,752 --> 00:00:15,158
So the same pull request also deletes the
docs team's line from CODEOWNERS, the file

3
00:00:15,198 --> 00:00:20,020
that maps path patterns to the users
and teams whom GitHub asks for review.

4
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:25,272
The reasoning: if the team is no longer
an owner, the team is no longer required.

5
00:00:25,312 --> 00:00:27,865
Does that work? Say yes or no, out loud.

6
00:00:32,705 --> 00:00:37,392
No. If it did, every required reviewer could
be removed by the person who needs the

7
00:00:37,432 --> 00:00:42,005
review. In this video you see why it doesn't
work, which Git command tells you so

8
00:00:42,045 --> 00:00:46,618
before you open the pull request, and what
you have to configure so that the file

9
00:00:46,658 --> 00:00:51,288
that names the reviewers is itself protected.
Watch for the moment in the terminal

10
00:00:51,328 --> 00:00:53,509
where that deleted line is still there.

11
00:00:55,349 --> 00:00:59,617
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:59,657 --> 00:01:05,337
In the last video you learned what a
CODEOWNERS file says: patterns, owners, last

13
00:01:05,377 --> 00:01:10,915
match wins. This video is about the file
in force. Five topics, in the order of

14
00:01:10,955 --> 00:01:12,539
sections 19.7 to 19.11.

15
00:01:12,879 --> 00:01:15,935
Which version of the file
applies to a pull request.

16
00:01:15,975 --> 00:01:19,507
How you protect the file itself
and the workflows directory.

17
00:01:19,547 --> 00:01:24,270
How to lay out ownership in a monorepo,
one repository that holds many projects.

18
00:01:24,310 --> 00:01:28,497
The newer "required reviewers" rule
and when it complements CODEOWNERS.

19
00:01:28,537 --> 00:01:32,843
And three documented instruments that
tell you how GitHub read your file.

20
00:01:33,183 --> 00:01:37,504
The local demonstration answers every
question that a clone can answer.

21
00:01:37,544 --> 00:01:42,418
The enforcement itself happens on GitHub,
and you'll prove it in Lab 23.2 with a

22
00:01:42,458 --> 00:01:43,339
second account.

23
00:01:45,179 --> 00:01:46,758
Here's what you'll walk away with.

24
00:01:47,098 --> 00:01:48,510
After this video you can:

25
00:01:48,850 --> 00:01:53,333
say from which branch the CODEOWNERS file
is read for a pull request and why that

26
00:01:53,373 --> 00:01:53,779
matters;

27
00:01:54,119 --> 00:01:58,893
explain why a pull request cannot remove
its own required reviewer by editing the

28
00:01:58,933 --> 00:01:59,190
file;

29
00:01:59,530 --> 00:02:04,207
protect CODEOWNERS and the workflows directory
and name the conditions that make the

30
00:02:04,247 --> 00:02:05,105
protection real;

31
00:02:05,445 --> 00:02:09,928
lay out ownership for a monorepo and
decide when the "required reviewers" rule

32
00:02:09,968 --> 00:02:11,957
replaces or complements CODEOWNERS;

33
00:02:12,297 --> 00:02:15,754
check the file for errors
before merging a change to it.

34
00:02:17,594 --> 00:02:22,650
The base branch decides. In one sentence:
a pull request is judged by the CODEOWNERS

35
00:02:22,690 --> 00:02:27,625
file on its base branch, so a pull request
that edits CODEOWNERS is reviewed under

36
00:02:27,665 --> 00:02:28,413
the old file.

37
00:02:28,753 --> 00:02:33,533
Two words first. The base branch is the
branch a pull request asks to merge into,

38
00:02:33,573 --> 00:02:38,235
and the head branch holds the commits it
offers. The documentation: "To trigger

39
00:02:38,275 --> 00:02:43,233
review requests, pull requests use the
version of CODEOWNERS from the base branch of

40
00:02:43,273 --> 00:02:44,304
the pull request."

41
00:02:44,644 --> 00:02:50,745
For a pull request from a fork, a second
repository on GitHub connected to the one

42
00:02:50,785 --> 00:02:54,939
it was made from, into upstream,
that's upstream's file.

43
00:02:54,979 --> 00:03:00,630
In a stack of pull requests, where each
targets the branch of the one below,

44
00:03:00,670 --> 00:03:06,397
CODEOWNERS is, in the documentation's
words, "evaluated from the stack base".

45
00:03:06,737 --> 00:03:11,208
Why is it designed like this? Because
the alternative has no security value.

46
00:03:11,248 --> 00:03:14,887
The head branch is written by
the person asking for the merge.

47
00:03:14,927 --> 00:03:19,754
If their copy of the file decided, the
control would be in the hands of the person

48
00:03:19,794 --> 00:03:24,325
it's meant to check. The base branch is
the state everyone already agreed to.

49
00:03:24,665 --> 00:03:29,432
There's a second consequence that people
meet more often than the first: a fix to

50
00:03:29,472 --> 00:03:32,815
CODEOWNERS takes effect one
merge later than they expect.

51
00:03:32,855 --> 00:03:37,148
The pull request that carries the fix
is still judged under the old file.

52
00:03:37,488 --> 00:03:42,217
Protecting the file. In one sentence: the
file that names the reviewers, and the

53
00:03:42,257 --> 00:03:47,165
directory that defines what automation runs,
need owners themselves, or anyone with

54
00:03:47,205 --> 00:03:50,087
write access can change
both in one pull request.

55
00:03:50,427 --> 00:03:55,004
GitHub's advice, quoted in the textbook:
"To protect a repository fully against

56
00:03:55,044 --> 00:03:59,562
unauthorized changes, you also need to
define an owner for the CODEOWNERS file

57
00:03:59,602 --> 00:04:04,471
itself." The most secure method, according
to the documentation, is to keep the file

58
00:04:04,511 --> 00:04:09,380
in the .github directory and to define an
owner either for the file or for the whole

59
00:04:09,420 --> 00:04:09,965
directory.

60
00:04:10,305 --> 00:04:14,772
Owning the whole .github/ directory
also covers .github/workflows/.

61
00:04:14,812 --> 00:04:20,288
A workflow file, the YAML file that tells
GitHub Actions what to run, decides what

62
00:04:20,328 --> 00:04:25,871
runs with the repository's token and secrets,
so a change to it is a change to your

63
00:04:25,911 --> 00:04:30,513
deployment and security posture.
Part 7 of this course is about that.

64
00:04:30,853 --> 00:04:35,848
Three conditions make the protection real.
The line must come last, so that no later

65
00:04:35,888 --> 00:04:40,584
pattern such as *.yaml takes workflow
files away. The code-owner option must be

66
00:04:40,624 --> 00:04:45,379
required by a rule on the default branch.
And the owning team must hold explicit

67
00:04:45,419 --> 00:04:50,055
write access. If one of the three is
missing, you have a line in a file and no

68
00:04:50,095 --> 00:04:50,535
control.

69
00:04:50,875 --> 00:04:55,768
Monorepos. In one sentence: in a monorepo
the file is the map of the organization,

70
00:04:55,808 --> 00:04:59,858
and its order has to follow the rule
"general first, specific last".

71
00:05:00,198 --> 00:05:04,399
Four blocks. A default, so that
somebody always owns a new directory.

72
00:05:04,439 --> 00:05:09,316
One block per component, anchored at the
root. Then the cross-cutting file types

73
00:05:09,356 --> 00:05:14,109
that must override the components. And
last, the files that control review and

74
00:05:14,149 --> 00:05:14,785
automation.

75
00:05:15,125 --> 00:05:19,768
Use the directory form for subtrees,
because dir/* covers one level only.

76
00:05:19,808 --> 00:05:25,093
Anchor component paths with a leading slash,
or router/ also matches a directory of

77
00:05:25,133 --> 00:05:30,032
that name inside another component.
Prefer teams to people, so that ownership

78
00:05:30,072 --> 00:05:35,421
survives holidays and departures. And one
caveat that the textbook attaches to block

79
00:05:35,461 --> 00:05:40,425
three: the patterns with two stars follow
the gitignore rules and aren't among

80
00:05:40,465 --> 00:05:45,685
GitHub's documented examples, except **/logs.
Confirm them with the instruments at

81
00:05:45,725 --> 00:05:47,225
the end of this section.

82
00:05:47,565 --> 00:05:53,069
Quick quiz. You need both the routing team
and the data team to approve one path.

83
00:05:53,109 --> 00:05:57,791
Where does that requirement go? A,
both teams on one CODEOWNERS line.

84
00:05:57,831 --> 00:06:00,050
B, a different rule. Your answer?

85
00:06:04,890 --> 00:06:09,727
B, a different rule. What the file can't
express: "both teams must approve", "two

86
00:06:09,767 --> 00:06:13,279
members of this team", or
"everyone except this directory".

87
00:06:13,319 --> 00:06:18,276
The first two belong to the next rule. The
third has only the documented workaround

88
00:06:18,316 --> 00:06:19,600
of an owner-less line.

89
00:06:19,940 --> 00:06:25,046
The required reviewers rule. In one
sentence: since February 2026 a ruleset can

90
00:06:25,086 --> 00:06:30,387
require approvals from named teams for file
patterns, with a count per team, which

91
00:06:30,427 --> 00:06:33,123
covers the cases CODEOWNERS can't express.

92
00:06:33,463 --> 00:06:38,400
The documentation: you can specify up to
15 different teams, and for each team a

93
00:06:38,440 --> 00:06:41,759
number of required approvals.
Counts run from 0 to 10.

94
00:06:41,799 --> 00:06:46,985
Zero adds the team "for visibility". The
team must have write permissions or higher.

95
00:06:47,025 --> 00:06:51,900
The rule isn't available on user-owned
repositories, because they don't contain

96
00:06:51,940 --> 00:06:56,441
teams. It became generally available
on the seventeenth of February 2026.

97
00:06:56,481 --> 00:07:01,667
And GitHub positions it with care: it
"augments CODEOWNERS files but doesn't replace

98
00:07:01,707 --> 00:07:02,040
them".

99
00:07:02,380 --> 00:07:07,492
Compare the two. CODEOWNERS lives in a file
in the repository and is reviewed like

100
00:07:07,532 --> 00:07:11,639
code. The rule lives in a ruleset
and is edited by administrators.

101
00:07:11,679 --> 00:07:16,163
CODEOWNERS accepts users, teams and
emails. The rule accepts teams only.

102
00:07:16,203 --> 00:07:19,367
With several owners in
CODEOWNERS any one suffices.

103
00:07:19,407 --> 00:07:22,886
In the rule each listed team
has its own required count.

104
00:07:22,926 --> 00:07:28,101
CODEOWNERS is one file per repository and
branch. An organization ruleset can cover

105
00:07:28,141 --> 00:07:32,625
many repositories. CODEOWNERS takes
effect when it's on the base branch.

106
00:07:32,665 --> 00:07:35,703
The rule takes effect when
the ruleset is active.

107
00:07:36,043 --> 00:07:41,614
One caveat stays attached. Chapter 18,
section 18.7 records an unresolved point: the

108
00:07:41,654 --> 00:07:46,891
documentation page and the REST schema
describe the pattern syntax of this rule

109
00:07:46,931 --> 00:07:49,696
differently. Test before you depend on it.

110
00:07:50,036 --> 00:07:53,471
Finding out what GitHub thinks.
Three documented instruments.

111
00:07:53,511 --> 00:07:57,915
First: when you navigate to the CODEOWNERS
file in your repository, errors are

112
00:07:57,955 --> 00:08:02,643
highlighted. Second: when you browse to a
file, hovering over the shield icon shows

113
00:08:02,683 --> 00:08:06,802
a tool tip with code ownership details,
for the branch you're looking at.

114
00:08:06,842 --> 00:08:11,416
Third: a REST endpoint, an address of
GitHub's API, lists the errors of the file,

115
00:08:11,456 --> 00:08:12,612
with an optional ref.

116
00:08:12,952 --> 00:08:17,309
All three are 🟢 SAFE: they read. They use
documented endpoints and weren't executed

117
00:08:17,349 --> 00:08:21,653
by the authors. Each error has a line, a
column, a kind, a message and sometimes a

118
00:08:21,693 --> 00:08:25,256
suggestion. The CLI has no dedicated
command for linting CODEOWNERS.

119
00:08:25,296 --> 00:08:29,706
The second command is the one to make a
habit: it asks for the errors of the file as

120
00:08:29,746 --> 00:08:32,196
it is on your branch,
before the change merges.

121
00:08:34,036 --> 00:08:38,871
Hold two copies of the file in your head,
one on each branch, and ask of every

122
00:08:38,911 --> 00:08:40,871
question: which copy answers it?

123
00:08:41,211 --> 00:08:44,583
The directory analogy from the
last video extends to this.

124
00:08:44,623 --> 00:08:49,467
A visitor to the building is announced
according to the directory that hangs in the

125
00:08:49,507 --> 00:08:54,056
lobby today. A visitor who brings a
corrected directory under the arm is still

126
00:08:54,096 --> 00:08:58,998
announced by the old one. The new one is
hung up only after someone who is listed on

127
00:08:59,038 --> 00:09:00,528
the old one has let it in.

128
00:09:00,868 --> 00:09:05,806
The analogy breaks where it did before: this
directory is read top to bottom and the

129
00:09:05,846 --> 00:09:09,244
last line that fits wins. And
it breaks in a second place.

130
00:09:09,284 --> 00:09:13,985
A lobby directory is one object. Here
there's one file per branch, and a release

131
00:09:14,025 --> 00:09:17,304
branch can legitimately have
different owners from main.

132
00:09:17,644 --> 00:09:23,061
When the copy on the base branch is the
control, the control has three ways to fail

133
00:09:23,101 --> 00:09:28,517
without anyone editing it in bad faith: a
later line takes the protected path away,

134
00:09:28,557 --> 00:09:33,908
the rule that makes owner review required
is missing or doesn't target the branch,

135
00:09:33,948 --> 00:09:39,298
or the owner has no explicit write access.
And it has two ways to be bypassed that

136
00:09:39,338 --> 00:09:43,177
no file can prevent: a bypass
actor, and an indirect merge.

137
00:09:43,217 --> 00:09:48,305
Those were covered in Chapter 17, section
17.13, and Chapter 18, section 18.5.

138
00:09:48,645 --> 00:09:53,511
Before the next picture, a prediction.
Two copies of the file, one on the base

139
00:09:53,551 --> 00:09:57,789
branch and one on the head branch,
with a pull request between them.

140
00:09:57,829 --> 00:10:01,626
From which copy do the review
requests come? Say it out loud.

141
00:10:07,966 --> 00:10:13,307
The arrow goes to the left box, the base
branch. The right box becomes the left box

142
00:10:13,347 --> 00:10:17,846
only by merging, and merging needs
the owners that the left box names.

143
00:10:19,686 --> 00:10:23,229
Into the lab. The situation:
the repository ticket-router.

144
00:10:23,269 --> 00:10:28,109
You're on a branch that changes four
paths. One of them is the CODEOWNERS file.

145
00:10:28,449 --> 00:10:30,783
Step 1: which file, on which branch.

146
00:10:31,123 --> 00:10:35,077
Notice what the command asks about:
origin/main, not your working tree.

147
00:10:35,117 --> 00:10:37,326
That's the whole lesson in one argument.

148
00:10:37,666 --> 00:10:43,270
Two files exist on main. The documented
order makes the one in .github/ the one that

149
00:10:43,310 --> 00:10:43,741
counts.

150
00:10:44,081 --> 00:10:46,204
Step 2: read the base version.

151
00:10:46,544 --> 00:10:50,079
Read it as you learned in the
last video: general first.

152
00:10:50,119 --> 00:10:55,313
The last line gives the whole .github/
directory to the repository administrators.

153
00:10:55,353 --> 00:10:59,207
Check the first of the three
conditions: is it the last line?

154
00:10:59,247 --> 00:10:59,590
It is.

155
00:10:59,930 --> 00:11:01,906
Step 3: the changed paths.

156
00:11:02,246 --> 00:11:06,903
Three dots, as on the Files changed tab
of a pull request: the changes since the

157
00:11:06,943 --> 00:11:07,548
merge base.

158
00:11:07,888 --> 00:11:13,471
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper. Four
paths. For each, which line of the base

159
00:11:13,511 --> 00:11:17,791
version decides? Write the four
lines down before you continue.

160
00:11:17,831 --> 00:11:21,494
One hint from the last video:
docs/* covers one level.

161
00:11:23,834 --> 00:11:26,603
Step 4: the pull request edits the file.

162
00:11:26,943 --> 00:11:31,985
The diff removes the docs line. Which of
the two grep commands will find a line, and

163
00:11:32,025 --> 00:11:34,828
which will exit with
status 1? Say it out loud.

164
00:11:39,668 --> 00:11:44,271
The base version still has the line,
at line 12. The head version doesn't.

165
00:11:44,311 --> 00:11:48,160
grep exits with 1. Review requests
come from the base version.

166
00:11:48,200 --> 00:11:53,367
So the docs team is requested for docs/README.md
although your branch deleted their

167
00:11:53,407 --> 00:11:58,637
line. That's the opening question, answered:
you can't remove a reviewer in the pull

168
00:11:58,677 --> 00:12:00,143
request that needs them.

169
00:12:00,483 --> 00:12:05,626
And look at what else this pull request
changes: .github/CODEOWNERS itself.

170
00:12:05,666 --> 00:12:09,635
Under the base version, who
owns that path? The last line.

171
00:12:09,675 --> 00:12:13,159
So the repository administrators
are requested too.

172
00:12:13,499 --> 00:12:19,484
Step 5: the lab replay. Replay
labs/run ch18/lab-23-2-codeowners.

173
00:12:19,524 --> 00:12:26,991
It asks the same questions in the order
of the lab, and then walks into a trap on

174
00:12:27,031 --> 00:12:27,733
purpose.

175
00:12:28,073 --> 00:12:32,617
The non-comment lines with their line
numbers: this is the form in which you reason

176
00:12:32,657 --> 00:12:33,667
about "last match".

177
00:12:34,007 --> 00:12:41,384
Now check your paper. .github/CODEOWNERS:
line 15. config/routing.yaml: line 9,

178
00:12:41,424 --> 00:12:46,737
platform and SRE. docs/README.md:
line 12, the docs team.

179
00:12:46,777 --> 00:12:54,154
And docs/runbooks/escalation.md: line 2
only, the platform team, because docs/*

180
00:12:54,194 --> 00:12:56,690
doesn't match nested files.

181
00:12:57,030 --> 00:13:01,225
One deletion in the file on your branch,
and it doesn't decide this pull request.

182
00:13:01,565 --> 00:13:06,349
Now the failure scenario of the lab. The
shortcut is tempting: strip the owners from

183
00:13:06,389 --> 00:13:10,311
the file and let Git match the
patterns as if they were ignore rules.

184
00:13:10,651 --> 00:13:16,115
Look at the first three lines of the
result. Git names line 2, the star, for

185
00:13:16,155 --> 00:13:20,460
config/routing.yaml, for
docs/README.md and for the runbook.

186
00:13:20,500 --> 00:13:25,167
Is that what GitHub is documented
to do? Compare with your paper.

187
00:13:27,507 --> 00:13:30,322
It isn't, and the recovery
snippet isolates why.

188
00:13:30,662 --> 00:13:36,124
With a catch-all line followed by *.yaml,
Git names line 2 for routing.yaml at the

189
00:13:36,164 --> 00:13:41,492
top level and line 1 for config/routing.yaml.
Git decided the second path at the

190
00:13:41,532 --> 00:13:45,249
directory config/, which the
first line already matches.

191
00:13:45,289 --> 00:13:50,684
That's the directory-first behavior of
gitignore, and CODEOWNERS is documented to

192
00:13:50,724 --> 00:13:56,320
assign owners per file. So the shortcut
gives wrong owners with complete confidence.

193
00:13:56,360 --> 00:14:00,010
Reason from the documented
rules and confirm on GitHub.

194
00:14:00,350 --> 00:14:04,856
Part B of Lab 23.2, on your practice
repository, in your normal shell.

195
00:14:04,896 --> 00:14:10,246
The interface changes. The lab text and the
linked documentation are the reference.

196
00:14:10,286 --> 00:14:15,182
No GitHub output was captured by the
authors. You need a second account or a

197
00:14:15,222 --> 00:14:15,766
teammate.

198
00:14:16,106 --> 00:14:19,982
The lab has five stages. You give
the second account write access.

199
00:14:20,022 --> 00:14:24,728
You add a CODEOWNERS file through a pull
request, and before merging you ask the

200
00:14:24,768 --> 00:14:29,237
errors endpoint about the file on your
branch. You add a second ruleset that

201
00:14:29,277 --> 00:14:30,839
requires code owner review.

202
00:14:31,179 --> 00:14:37,061
That's a gh api call with POST, which Chapter
18 labels 🔴 DANGEROUS, and whose five

203
00:14:37,101 --> 00:14:42,840
answers you heard in video 134: create,
read back with gh ruleset check main, and

204
00:14:42,880 --> 00:14:48,618
disable or delete to recover. Then you
open a pull request that touches /router/,

205
00:14:48,658 --> 00:14:50,759
which the second account owns.

206
00:14:51,099 --> 00:14:55,745
On screen, name the controls by function.
In the pull request, find the list of

207
00:14:55,785 --> 00:14:59,007
requested reviewers and see
whether the owner is in it.

208
00:14:59,047 --> 00:15:02,092
Find the merge box and read
what it says is missing.

209
00:15:02,132 --> 00:15:06,956
The merge attempt is expected to be refused.
Then the second account approves, and

210
00:15:06,996 --> 00:15:11,642
you read the review decision again and
merge. What you have then proved: a code

211
00:15:11,682 --> 00:15:16,150
owner's approval is required when a rule
says so, and the base branch's file

212
00:15:16,190 --> 00:15:16,625
decides.

213
00:15:18,465 --> 00:15:20,113
Five mistakes to watch for.

214
00:15:20,453 --> 00:15:24,859
Expecting an edit to CODEOWNERS to apply
to the pull request that makes it.

215
00:15:24,899 --> 00:15:29,306
Root cause: review requests use the
version of the file on the base branch.

216
00:15:29,646 --> 00:15:34,794
Leaving CODEOWNERS itself without an
owner. Root cause: the file is an ordinary

217
00:15:34,834 --> 00:15:39,720
tracked file, so anyone with write
access can rewrite it unless a line owns

218
00:15:39,760 --> 00:15:42,544
/.github/ and a rule
requires owner review.

219
00:15:42,884 --> 00:15:48,537
Placing the /.github/ line anywhere but
last. Root cause: a later pattern such as

220
00:15:48,577 --> 00:15:52,332
*.yaml matches workflow files
and replaces the owners.

221
00:15:52,672 --> 00:15:57,782
Listing one person as sole owner. Root
cause: authors cannot approve their own pull

222
00:15:57,822 --> 00:16:02,808
requests, so the owner's own changes have
nobody who can satisfy the requirement.

223
00:16:03,148 --> 00:16:08,044
Testing the file with git check-ignore. Root
cause: Git's matcher stops at a matched

224
00:16:08,084 --> 00:16:10,982
directory, and CODEOWNERS
assigns owners per file.

225
00:16:12,822 --> 00:16:18,108
Now, out of the lab. A team that serves
models keeps its deployment workflow in

226
00:16:18,148 --> 00:16:23,569
.github/workflows/. CODEOWNERS gives
/.github/ to the platform team, and the file

227
00:16:23,609 --> 00:16:29,030
has had that line for a year. A change to
the deployment workflow merges with one

228
00:16:29,070 --> 00:16:31,255
approval from a product engineer.

229
00:16:31,595 --> 00:16:35,222
Work through the three
conditions. Is the line last?

230
00:16:35,262 --> 00:16:41,005
Someone appended a *.yaml line for the SRE
team three months ago, and the workflow

231
00:16:41,045 --> 00:16:44,179
file is a YAML file, so
SRE became its owner.

232
00:16:44,519 --> 00:16:48,128
Does a rule require code owner
review on the default branch?

233
00:16:48,168 --> 00:16:52,385
Check the rules page. Does the owning
team have explicit write access?

234
00:16:52,425 --> 00:16:57,251
Ask the errors endpoint. In this case the
first condition failed, and the errors

235
00:16:57,291 --> 00:17:01,569
endpoint has nothing to report, because
nothing in the file is invalid.

236
00:17:01,609 --> 00:17:04,915
The file says exactly what
its last matching line says.

237
00:17:05,255 --> 00:17:08,472
The repair is to move the
/.github/ line to the end.

238
00:17:08,512 --> 00:17:13,484
And remember the timing: the pull request
that moves it is judged by the file on

239
00:17:13,524 --> 00:17:18,747
main, so it needs the approval of whoever
owns .github/CODEOWNERS under the current,

240
00:17:18,787 --> 00:17:19,499
wrong order.

241
00:17:21,339 --> 00:17:27,081
Your turn. Do Lab 23.2, "CODEOWNERS
enforcement with a second account", in

242
00:17:27,121 --> 00:17:31,456
lab-manual/m23-governance.md.
Part A runs in labs/shell.

243
00:17:31,496 --> 00:17:37,941
The IDs there differ from the ones in this
video. Part B runs in your normal shell.

244
00:17:38,281 --> 00:17:43,263
In Part A, after the four observation steps,
stop and predict on paper, for each of

245
00:17:43,303 --> 00:17:47,800
the four changed paths: which line
decides, and which owners are requested.

246
00:17:47,840 --> 00:17:52,822
In Part B, before each merge attempt, predict
whether GitHub will allow it and what

247
00:17:52,862 --> 00:17:54,940
the merge box will name as missing.

248
00:17:55,280 --> 00:17:59,748
The challenge is Exercise
23.8, ""Main is protected.

249
00:17:59,788 --> 00:18:05,644
How did a force push get through?"",
in exercises/m19-m25-github.md.

250
00:18:09,484 --> 00:18:14,293
Answer out loud first. A strong answer names
the rule and its source: which version

251
00:18:14,333 --> 00:18:18,616
of the file GitHub uses, and that this
is GitHub's behavior and not Git's.

252
00:18:18,656 --> 00:18:23,348
It explains the design reason, not only
the fact. It shows the local evidence you

253
00:18:23,388 --> 00:18:26,444
would look at, with the
branch named in each command.

254
00:18:26,784 --> 00:18:31,580
It adds the consequence in the other
direction, for a legitimate fix to the file.

255
00:18:31,620 --> 00:18:36,178
And it says what must also be true for
the control to hold: who owns the file

256
00:18:36,218 --> 00:18:39,283
itself, and which rule makes
that ownership binding.

257
00:18:41,123 --> 00:18:45,679
Let's land this. The developer from the
opening deleted a line and removed nobody,

258
00:18:45,719 --> 00:18:48,146
because the copy on the
base branch decides.

259
00:18:48,486 --> 00:18:50,146
You should now be able to say:

260
00:18:50,486 --> 00:18:55,338
A pull request is judged by the CODEOWNERS
file on its base branch; an edit takes

261
00:18:55,378 --> 00:18:56,727
effect one merge later.

262
00:18:57,067 --> 00:19:01,831
The file and the workflows directory need
an owner, and that protection is real only

263
00:19:01,871 --> 00:19:06,577
if the line is last, a rule requires owner
review, and the owning team has explicit

264
00:19:06,617 --> 00:19:07,320
write access.

265
00:19:07,660 --> 00:19:13,162
In a monorepo: default first, components
next, cross-cutting patterns after them,

266
00:19:13,202 --> 00:19:14,188
/.github/ last.

267
00:19:14,528 --> 00:19:18,896
CODEOWNERS means "any one owner"; where
several teams or counts are needed, the

268
00:19:18,936 --> 00:19:21,072
required reviewers rule complements it.

269
00:19:21,412 --> 00:19:25,103
The errors endpoint with a ref
tests an edit before it merges.

270
00:19:26,943 --> 00:19:31,384
Read sections 19.7 to 19.14 of Chapter 19.

271
00:19:31,724 --> 00:19:37,200
Today you asked a clone which CODEOWNERS
file counts, and you saw why the file can't

272
00:19:37,240 --> 00:19:42,650
be edited around. Before the next video,
ask a repository of yours which CODEOWNERS

273
00:19:42,690 --> 00:19:48,165
file exists on origin/main. Next time:
signatures. What is signed, by which program,

274
00:19:48,205 --> 00:19:53,418
and SSH signing end to end. Until then,
look at the state first and type second.

275
00:19:53,458 --> 00:19:54,994
See you in the next one.
